                      <td>{s.dr}</td>
                      <td>{s.da}</td>
                      <td>{s.pa}</td>
                      <td>{s.ur}</td>
                      <td>{s.traffic}</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))}
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </figure>

            <h2 id="pitch">The pitch that works for AI editors</h2>
            <ol>
              <li><strong>Lead with the artifact.</strong> "We open-sourced a 2B model that matches Llama on X" or "here is a benchmark of 8 RAG frameworks on the same corpus." The thing you built earns the read.</li>
              <li><strong>Show the code runs.</strong> A repo, a Colab, a reproduction script. Applied-ML editors reject claims they cannot check.</li>
              <li><strong>Pitch the section.</strong> "For your applied research coverage" or "for the tutorials series" shows you read the publication.</li>
              <li><strong>Match the format.</strong> News wants a tight story. Tutorials sites want a structured how-to with code. Research blogs want a paper-grade write-up.</li>
              <li><strong>Bring the assets.</strong> Charts, a dataset, model weights, an eval harness.</li>
              <li><strong>Disclose funding or a commercial interest</strong> in the model or tool you are writing about.</li>
            </ol>
            <div class="callout"><p><strong>The test:</strong> would an editor run this if your company did not exist? If yes, pitch it. If it is a walkthrough of your API, it goes in the bin.</p></div>

            <h2 id="mistakes">What not to do</h2>
            <ul class="check-list">
              <li>Pitch a "what is machine learning" explainer. Every site has ten, and it will not rank.</li>
              <li>Send benchmarks you will not share the code for.</li>
              <li>Submit an LLM-written post about LLMs with no experiments and no author who did the work.</li>
              <li>Put exact-match anchors ("best AI writing tool") on links back to your product.</li>
              <li>Overclaim in the title. AI editors and readers punish hype hard.</li>
            </ul>
            <div class="callout"><p><strong>Bottom line:</strong> in AI, one placement on a respected applied-ML outlet with working code beats a dozen links from thin "AI news" sites. Use recent publishing activity and the traffic column to find the real ones.</p></div>
          </div>

